PROCESS AND PROGRAM

A program is a set of instructions written in a programming language to perform a specific task. It is a passive entity that is stored on secondary storage such as a hard disk or SSD. A program does not do anything by itself. It becomes active only when it is loaded into memory and executed.

A process is a program in execution. It is an active entity. When a program is loaded into main memory and starts running, the operating system creates a process for it. A process includes not only the program code but also its current activity, such as the value of the program counter, registers, stack, heap, and allocated resources.

In simple words, a program is like a recipe written on paper, while a process is the actual cooking happening using that recipe.

Key points about Program
A program is a static entity
It is stored on disk
It does not need CPU time
It does not have states
Multiple processes can be created from the same program

Key points about Process
A process is a dynamic entity
It resides in main memory
It requires CPU time for execution
It has states like new, ready, running, waiting, and terminated
Each process is independent, even if created from the same program

Difference between Program and Process

Program
Passive
Stored on secondary storage
No execution
No states
Single copy on disk

Process
Active
Stored in main memory
Under execution
Has multiple states
Multiple instances can exist

Example
Consider a text editor like Notepad.
The Notepad application stored on disk is a program.
When you open Notepad, the operating system creates a process for it.
If you open Notepad twice, the same program creates two different processes.

Conclusion
A program is a static set of instructions, while a process is the active execution of those instructions. The operating system manages processes to ensure proper execution, resource allocation, and multitasking.
